::ALG2_U05_L06_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find the inverse of f(x)=x+9. {
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x-9#Correct. Subtract 9 to undo adding 9.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x+9#This repeats the original.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=9x#This does not undo addition.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/(x+9)#This is reciprocal notation.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find the inverse of f(x)=x-7. {
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x-7#This repeats the original.
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x+7#Correct. Add 7 to undo subtracting 7.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=7x#This does not undo subtraction.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/(x-7)#This is reciprocal.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find the inverse of f(x)=4x. {
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=4x#This repeats the original.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x-4#This undoes addition.
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x/4#Correct. Divide by 4 to undo multiplying by 4.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/(4x)#This is reciprocal.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find the inverse of f(x)=x/5. {
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x/5#This repeats the original.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x-5#This undoes addition.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/(x/5)#This is reciprocal.
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=5x#Correct. Multiply by 5 to undo dividing by 5.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find the inverse of f(x)=2x+3. {
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=(x-3)/2#Correct. Undo +3, then undo multiplication by 2.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=2x-3#This does not reverse operations.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=(x+3)/2#The sign is wrong.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/(2x+3)#This is reciprocal.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find the inverse of f(x)=5x-10. {
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=5x+10#This does not reverse operations.
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=(x+10)/5#Correct. Undo -10 by adding 10, then divide by 5.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=(x-10)/5#The sign is wrong.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/(5x-10)#This is reciprocal.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find the inverse of f(x)=(x+2)/3. {
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=3x+2#The sign is wrong.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=(x-2)/3#This does not reverse the operations.
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=3x-2#Correct. Multiply by 3, then subtract 2.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/((x+2)/3)#This is reciprocal.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find the inverse of f(x)=3x-1. {
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=(x-1)/3#The sign is wrong.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=3x+1#This does not reverse operations.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/(3x-1)#This is reciprocal.
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=(x+1)/3#Correct. Add 1, then divide by 3.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Which composition verifies f and g are inverses? {
= f(g(x))=x and g(f(x))=x#Correct. Both directions must return x.
~ f(x)+g(x)=0#This checks additive opposites, not inverses.
~ f(x)g(x)=1#This checks reciprocals in special cases.
~ f(x)=g(x)#Inverse functions usually differ.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x+2 and g(x)=x-2, what is f(g(x))? {
~ x+4#This adds both shifts.
= x#Correct. Substitute x-2 into x+2.
~ x-4#This subtracts both shifts.
~ 2x#This adds functions.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=2x and g(x)=x/2, what is g(f(x))? {
~ 4x#This multiplies instead of composing.
~ x/4#This uses the wrong order.
= x#Correct. Substitute 2x into x/2.
~ 2#This cancels x incorrectly.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>What graph relationship do inverse functions have? {
~ Reflection across x-axis#That changes output signs.
~ Reflection across y-axis#That changes input signs.
~ Same graph always#Inverses are not always identical.
= Reflection across y=x#Correct. Inverse functions switch x- and y-values.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>What happens to domain and range for inverse functions? {
= They switch roles#Correct. Inputs and outputs reverse.
~ They disappear#Domain and range still exist.
~ They are always all real numbers#This ignores restrictions; inverse domain and range depend on the original function.
~ They stay unrelated#They are connected by reversal.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>What does one-to-one mean? {
~ Each input has two outputs#That is not a function.
= Each output comes from exactly one input#Correct. This allows an inverse function without ambiguity.
~ Every output is zero#That is unrelated.
~ The graph is always linear#Nonlinear functions can be one-to-one on a domain.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Why might a quadratic need a restricted domain to have an inverse? {
~ Because quadratics never have inverses#They can have inverses on restricted domains.
~ Because outputs are always negative#Not true for all quadratics.
= Otherwise some outputs come from two inputs#Correct. A full quadratic is not one-to-one.
~ Because inverse means reciprocal#Inverse function does not mean reciprocal.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>If f(3)=10, what point is on f<sup>-1</sup>? {
~ (3,10)#That is on f, not necessarily f inverse.
~ (-3,-10)#Inverse does not negate coordinates.
~ (1/3,1/10)#Inverse does not mean reciprocal coordinates.
= (10,3)#Correct. Inverse functions switch input and output.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>If f<sup>-1</sup>(8)=2, what is true about f? {
= f(2)=8#Correct. Inverse notation reverses input and output.
~ f(8)=2#This reverses the statement.
~ f(2)=1/8#Inverse is not reciprocal.
~ f(8)=1/2#Inverse is not reciprocal.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find inverse of f(x)=x/2+6. {
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=2x+12#The sign is wrong.
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=2x-12#Correct. Subtract 6, then multiply by 2.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x/2-6#This does not reverse operations.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/(x/2+6)#This is reciprocal.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find inverse of f(x)=7- x. {
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x-7#This does not undo 7-x.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x+7#This is not correct.
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=7-x#Correct. This function reverses itself.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/(7-x)#This is reciprocal.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is correct? {
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/f(x) always#That is reciprocal notation, not inverse function notation.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x) means negative f#Negative f is -f(x).
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x) means f minus 1#That would be f(x)-1.
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x) means inverse function#Correct. The notation names the function that reverses f.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Which pair are inverses? {
= f(x)=x+4 and g(x)=x-4#Correct. Each function undoes the other.
~ f(x)=x+4 and g(x)=x+4#They do not undo each other.
~ f(x)=2x and g(x)=x+2#These do not undo each other.
~ f(x)=x<sup>2</sup> and g(x)=x/2#These are not inverses.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Which pair are inverses? {
~ f(x)=3x and g(x)=3x#They do not undo each other.
= f(x)=3x and g(x)=x/3#Correct. Multiplication by 3 and division by 3 undo each other.
~ f(x)=x+3 and g(x)=3x#These do not undo each other.
~ f(x)=x-3 and g(x)=x/3#These do not undo each other.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>How do you find an inverse algebraically? {
~ Square both sides always#That is not the inverse procedure.
~ Multiply by -1#That only reflects.
= Switch x and y, then solve for y#Correct. This reverses the input-output relationship.
~ Replace x with 0#That evaluates an intercept.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>What does an inverse function do in context? {
~ It always predicts a larger value#Not necessarily.
~ It changes every value to its reciprocal#That is reciprocal, not inverse.
~ It removes the need for units#Context still needs units.
= It reverses the relationship to find the original input from an output#Correct. Inverse functions answer the reverse question.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=2x+8, what is f<sup>-1</sup>(10)? {
= 1#Correct. Solve (10-8)/2.
~ 28#This evaluates f(10).
~ 9#This subtracts 1 incorrectly.
~ 5#This divides by 2 but ignores +8.
}

